# Keep the build context to just the sources the image builds from. Everything
# here is either regenerated inside the image or irrelevant to it — and a stale
# host build/ directory leaking in is a genuine source of "works locally,
# broken in the container".
.git
.gradle
build
*/build
kotlin-js-store
.claude
.idea
local.properties

# Planning + instruction documents, and the local dev scripts. They belong in
# the repo, not in the image. (The Dockerfile COPYs specific paths, so these
# were never going to land in the image regardless — excluding them keeps the
# build context small, and a smaller context is a faster build.)
docs/
scripts/*

# …except the entrypoint, which is genuinely part of the image: it chowns the
# mounted volume and drops to the unprivileged user before exec'ing the JVM.
# See the Dockerfile, and the script's own header for why that has to happen at
# runtime rather than at build time.
#
# Note `scripts/*` above rather than `scripts/`: Docker will not re-include a
# file from a directory that has itself been excluded, so the negation below is
# silently ineffective against a bare `scripts/` and the COPY fails with "file
# not found" while the file is plainly right there.
!scripts/container-entrypoint.sh
